  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x}\\,?\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-1=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}}{4(ty)-(tx)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}}{4ty-tx} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3})}{t(4y-x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{3}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$",
